WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.701
-Our next guest is a Tony-nominated

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.901
and Grammy Award-winning actor you know from "Mindhunter,"

00:00:04.901 --> 00:00:08.234
"The Matrix Resurrections," and his work on Broadway

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:09.701
in "Hamilton" and "Spring Awakening."

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:12.167
He stars in the new film "Knock at the Cabin,"

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:13.701
which is in theaters this Friday.

00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:15.033
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.501
-I don't know what's going on here,

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:19.934
but where are the keys to the truck you guys came in?

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:23.767
I'm taking my family, and we're leaving.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.501
♪♪

00:00:26.501 --> 00:00:31.434
-Do you really think that everything that happened today,

00:00:31.434 --> 00:00:35.400
everything we've seen...

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.167
do you really think it's all just a coincidence?

00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:40.901
-Yes.

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:43.801
I think it's all a coincidence.

00:00:43.801 --> 00:00:46.434
Some horrible, unexplainable coincidence.

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:50.801
Or it has to be a trick! I have to believe that!

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:54.033
-You already don't believe that.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:57.667
-Please welcome back to the show Jonathan Groff, everybody.

00:00:57.667 --> 00:00:59.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:07.767
♪♪

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:15.968
♪♪

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:17.701
How are you?

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:19.067
-I'm so good. How are you?

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:21.100
-It's been too long since you've been here.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.000
-I know. I have to tell you that --

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.267
You don't know this,

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:27.167
but we spent a lot of COVID together, you and I.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:28.534
-Oh, yeah? -Yeah.

00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:30.834
You were like my special friend.

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:34.100
I couldn't really watch the news in those first several months.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.400
-Yeah, it was a tough time. -I would have a drink

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.634
and a protein bar, and I would go to my bed,

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.734
and I would sit and I would watch you.

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.467
And I can't thank you enough for getting me through that time.

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:46.234
You're just, like, the best. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:46.234 --> 00:01:48.033
-That's so nice of you to say. -You're just the best.

00:01:48.033 --> 00:01:52.033
-It was very special knowing that people were protein-barring

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:54.601
and drinking their way through it with me.

00:01:54.601 --> 00:01:57.701
And then I will say I introduced "Frozen" to my kids,

00:01:57.701 --> 00:02:00.133
and that was a very tricky time to have kids.

00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:02.968
And so I -- I had great love for you

00:02:02.968 --> 00:02:06.267
that then obviously hit a point, and then it just --

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:08.467
-Turned into resentment. -Yeah.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.934
Obviously, it's nice to see you and put a face to you again.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.767
But your voice -- No.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:17.033
-"What are you talking about, Seth?"

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:20.200
-This is so cool to see you in an M. Night movie

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.367
because obviously you've done darker stuff,

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:25.434
right, like "Mindhunter," but this is like full horror.

00:02:25.434 --> 00:02:26.801
Never done it before.

00:02:26.801 --> 00:02:30.300
-Full, tearful horror, yeah. -Was it --

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.300
I mean, he must be such an exciting person to work with.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.200
-Yeah, I was so excited to get

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.734
to even audition for him and meet him online.

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:40.501
And working with him was amazing.

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:42.534
He's incredibly specific.

00:02:42.534 --> 00:02:46.033
It was incredibly intense in that cabin.

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:48.000
But we -- we really had so much fun and kind of formed

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.667
a little family with our cast. It was great.

00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:51.300
-That's a really cool thing to go through.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.734
Do you have -- I mean, I feel like I know mine.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:55.667
Do you remember your first, like, M. Night moment

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.501
where you're like, "Oh, my God"? Like, a film of his?

00:02:58.501 --> 00:02:59.968
-"I see dead people." -Yeah.

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:01.501
-I mean, that was the one. It's a classic.

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:03.634
-Yeah. -Yeah, "The Sixth Sense."

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:06.200
-It's really amazing that all these years later,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.367
he's still a person that you want to go see his film

00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.968
because you remember that first twist being so good.

00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:12.334
-Yeah.

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:15.534
He's one of those few directors where their name alone

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:18.167
brings people into the theater. It's pretty cool.

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:21.100
-Did you -- Were you a fan of horror growing up?

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:25.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:27.901
Maybe. Maybe not.

00:03:27.901 --> 00:03:29.267
-Yeah. I feel like --

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:31.467
I was a little bit of an outsider in middle school.

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:32.834
-Yeah. -I feel like the genre

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:34.801
of horror tends to grav-- As an outsider,

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:36.968
I feel, like, gravitated for me.

00:03:36.968 --> 00:03:38.534
It was "Scream." -Yeah.

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:43.100
-Yeah. I had the mask. I had the voice changer.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.267
-And you brought it to school?

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:46.534
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.033
Okay, yeah.

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:49.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.434 --> 00:03:53.000
-I would call people and be like, "Surprise, Sidney."

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.367
[ Laughter ] -Yeah.

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.901
You're begging for outsider once you start doing that.

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:01.968
Now, how old are your nieces now?

00:04:01.968 --> 00:04:05.901
-Oh, my gosh. They are...

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:08.234
about to be eight and six.

00:04:08.234 --> 00:04:11.000
-Okay. So this is not -- This --

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.701
"Knock at the Cabin" is not for them.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:15.601
-This is not for them. My parents are seeing it --

00:04:15.601 --> 00:04:17.834
Or they just have seen it, I guess, tonight.

00:04:17.834 --> 00:04:19.534
-Oh, exciting. -Which is really exciting.

00:04:19.534 --> 00:04:21.133
My nieces will not be seeing it,

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:25.901
although we did have a really adorable 8-year-old.

00:04:25.901 --> 00:04:28.234
She's the exact same age as my niece.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:29.901
-Who's in the film. -Yes.

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:31.868
-Ben Aldridge and I play husbands,

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:34.234
and Kristen is our daughter.

00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:36.367
And we loved her so much.

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:40.334
She is an extraordinary, extraordinary young girl,

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:41.934
and she kills it in the movie.

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:43.501
And we took her to see "Wicked."

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:46.200
[ Audience awws ] -Now, this is pretty exciting.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.167
This was her first Broadway show, correct?

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:49.534
-This was her -- -Okay.

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:51.334
-This was her third Broadway show.

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:52.534
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:52.534 --> 00:04:53.834
-She'd seen "Aladdin" and "Hamilton."

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.167
-She did not see "Hamilton."

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:57.167
Obviously she was a bit too young to see --

00:04:57.167 --> 00:04:59.901
-She was born the year I was in "Hamilton."

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:01.467
[ Laughter ] Yeah, she was --

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.467
-That speaks to what a hit "Hamilton" is.

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:05.400
-That's so true. -That you can be in it

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.400
and she can be that old. -Yeah.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.868
I mean -- And "Wicked" was 20 years old.

00:05:09.868 --> 00:05:12.334
-And so what did she -- -And still hitting, by the way.

00:05:12.334 --> 00:05:14.634
"Wicked." -Still hitting?

00:05:14.634 --> 00:05:16.133
-It's still hitting.

00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:18.601
-And, now, how long had it been since you'd seen "Wicked"?

00:05:18.601 --> 00:05:21.267
Because I imagine there had been some air there for you, right?

00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:23.901
-You know, I realized when we brought her

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:26.300
that actually I brought her to "Wicked"

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:27.701
so that I could see "Wicked" again.

00:05:27.701 --> 00:05:28.968
-Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:30.434
-That was like the tenth time I'd seen "Wicked."

00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:32.033
-That's great. And it's still banging.

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:33.534
-It's still banging, and I remember

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:36.534
waiting at the stage door for Idina's autograph.

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:38.634
-Wow! As a -- -I was in high school.

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.000
-That's really exciting.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.767
-It was very exciting. -Did you get it?

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.467
Did you officially -- Did you succeed?

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:44.834
-I didn't get it. -Wow.

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:47.434
Have you brought this up with her in person since?

00:05:47.434 --> 00:05:48.601
-I think I did.

00:05:48.601 --> 00:05:49.968
-Yeah. -Yeah.

00:05:49.968 --> 00:05:52.801
-Well, hopefully she's learned from it.

00:05:52.801 --> 00:05:55.033
It you walk by anybody with a playbill,

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.834
just know one day they might make it.

00:05:57.834 --> 00:06:00.534
Now, what was her -- Tell me her name again.

00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:01.934
I'm sorry. -Kristen.

00:06:01.934 --> 00:06:03.801
-And Kristen -- what was her take on "Wicked"?

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:05.434
-She had some notes.

00:06:05.434 --> 00:06:07.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.434 --> 00:06:12.634
-Very precocious. -Very precocious.

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:14.968
She was like, "Musicals are weird."

00:06:14.968 --> 00:06:17.067
-Well. -I was like, "They are."

00:06:17.067 --> 00:06:19.934
She was like, "I think I prefer movies to musicals."

00:06:19.934 --> 00:06:23.501
But then I kept seeing her kind of catching herself laughing.

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:25.200
-Yeah. -She's at that kind of

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.300
precocious 8-year-old age where she's a little resistant,

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.667
but then I could see her getting transported by the show.

00:06:30.667 --> 00:06:32.133
It's incredible.

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:33.868
-You mentioned your parents are seeing it tonight.

00:06:33.868 --> 00:06:35.968
Do you have any expec-- Do they like this genre,

00:06:35.968 --> 00:06:38.267
or is this a little out of their -- Okay.

00:06:38.267 --> 00:06:41.400
So are you -- I mean, are you a little worried about, like,

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.167
scaring the bejesus out of your parents?

00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:47.534
-My parents have seen my, like, naked butt on Broadway.

00:06:47.534 --> 00:06:48.701
-Yeah.

00:06:48.701 --> 00:06:50.033
-You know, in "Spring Awakening."

00:06:50.033 --> 00:06:53.300
So watching me cry and talk about the apocalypse --

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:54.868
[ Laughter ]

00:06:54.868 --> 00:06:57.100
I think they'll be okay comparatively.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:58.367
-Right.

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:00.100
Everything's uphill from "Spring Awakening."

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.767
-Yes, or, like, with talking to serial killers in "Mindhunter."

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.300
This is like, you know --

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.200
I think they'll be able to suspend their disbelief.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.868
My mom really had to convince my dad to go.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:11.868
-Okay. Gotcha. -He's not really a movie guy,

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:14.267
so I can't wait to hear his review.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:15.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:18.868
He's like -- grew up on a dairy farm as a Mennonite,

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:20.534
so I'm going to be interested to see

00:07:20.534 --> 00:07:22.667
what he thinks of Night's movie.

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:26.133
-And does he shoot straight with his feedback?

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:27.601
-Yeah. -Okay.

00:07:27.601 --> 00:07:30.934
-Yeah. He would prefer to be watching football, but...

00:07:30.934 --> 00:07:32.200
-Well, there's no game tonight, so --

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:35.334
-No. I didn't even know that. Yeah.

00:07:35.334 --> 00:07:37.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:41.300
-Now, this is really exciting. It's coming to Broadway.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.200
You just did it here off Broadway.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.534
"Merrily We Roll Along."

00:07:44.534 --> 00:07:45.601
-Yes. -Very famous Sondheim --

00:07:45.601 --> 00:07:47.200
[ Cheers and applause ] There we go.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.767
That's the kind of crowd we bring.

00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.634
-This is -- I got to say.

00:07:50.634 --> 00:07:53.734
"Merrily We Roll Along" is a famous flop from 1981.

00:07:53.734 --> 00:07:55.334
-Famous flop? -Yeah, they only ran for

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:57.901
16 real performances on Broadway.

00:07:57.901 --> 00:07:59.934
And so the fact that, like, there's energy about

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:01.767
"Merrily We Roll Along" is so satisfying,

00:08:01.767 --> 00:08:03.434
even that you applauded for the title.

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:04.667
Thank you so much.

00:08:04.667 --> 00:08:06.667
It's an amazing show,

00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:09.000
and we were off-Broadway at New York Theatre Workshop,

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.734
and we're moving to Broadway this fall.

00:08:10.734 --> 00:08:12.801
We can't wait. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:12.801 --> 00:08:16.601
-So I feel like I knew that it was this famous flop,

00:08:16.601 --> 00:08:19.601
and I did not know how it was -- And excuse my ignorance.

00:08:19.601 --> 00:08:22.434
Within the community of people who like musicals,

00:08:22.434 --> 00:08:25.067
was it always this sense of, "This deserves another shot,

00:08:25.067 --> 00:08:27.400
this is way better than it got credit for"?

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.901
-I think it felt like an incredible score.

00:08:29.901 --> 00:08:32.367
Stephen Sondheim did the music and lyrics.

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:36.033
And it was just one of those shows that never quite worked

00:08:36.033 --> 00:08:38.901
or people thought it never quite worked.

00:08:38.901 --> 00:08:42.667
Our director, Maria Friedman, who is based in London

00:08:42.667 --> 00:08:44.601
and did this ten years ago in London,

00:08:44.601 --> 00:08:45.934
directed a production of it.

00:08:45.934 --> 00:08:47.701
She really believed in the piece,

00:08:47.701 --> 00:08:51.100
and we're doing it as written, the book and the lyrics,

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.734
and it went really well off-Broadway,

00:08:53.734 --> 00:08:55.801
so we're excited to bring it to Broadway.

00:08:55.801 --> 00:08:58.067
-And how long have you been off-Broadway yourself?

00:08:58.067 --> 00:08:59.334
Like, what was the last time --

00:08:59.334 --> 00:09:00.934
Has it been, like, longer than you've been here?

00:09:00.934 --> 00:09:03.033
Six years, seven years? -Oh, my God, yeah.

00:09:03.033 --> 00:09:06.200
I did "Little Shop of Horrors" in 2019.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.968
This is the first show I've done

00:09:07.968 --> 00:09:11.434
since the theater was closed for the pandemic.

00:09:11.434 --> 00:09:12.834
I could not stop crying

00:09:12.834 --> 00:09:14.734
throughout rehearsal, throughout the --

00:09:14.734 --> 00:09:16.767
It was me in the clip from the Night movie.

00:09:16.767 --> 00:09:19.133
I was just always like --

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:20.934
About to sing, crying.

00:09:20.934 --> 00:09:23.501
It was really emotional and felt so amazing to be back.

00:09:23.501 --> 00:09:25.300
And even to see "Wicked," just, like --

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.734
It's still not lost on me from being, like, with your --

00:09:29.734 --> 00:09:32.167
with you on my iPhone at night, drinking alcohol

00:09:32.167 --> 00:09:34.868
and eating protein bars to, like, being on Broadway,

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:37.133
seeing theater, being with a live audience here.

00:09:37.133 --> 00:09:38.467
-Yeah. -It's just like --

00:09:38.467 --> 00:09:41.634
I'll never forget how much I missed it, yeah.

00:09:41.634 --> 00:09:44.934
-It's very exciting to be in front of a live audience.

00:09:44.934 --> 00:09:46.801
It's very exciting to be in a live audience.

00:09:46.801 --> 00:09:48.868
-Totally. -Like, sitting and watching

00:09:48.868 --> 00:09:50.334
things again, it is such a --

00:09:50.334 --> 00:09:51.934
And especially being in the city

00:09:51.934 --> 00:09:53.968
where I feel like that community, you know --

00:09:53.968 --> 00:09:56.434
There's so many different places to go out and see that.

00:09:56.434 --> 00:09:57.667
-And it's the best.

00:09:57.667 --> 00:09:59.868
Like, New York is the capital of theater,

00:09:59.868 --> 00:10:03.367
and the best people do their best work here.

00:10:03.367 --> 00:10:07.100
And, yeah, I missed being in the audience just as much,

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.367
if not more than I missed being onstage.

00:10:09.367 --> 00:10:11.267
-And so this is -- So, when do you go back

00:10:11.267 --> 00:10:13.167
into rehearsals for "Merrily" for Broadway?

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:14.834
Like, how much time off do you have?

00:10:14.834 --> 00:10:16.868
-Yeah, we have like six or seven months off.

00:10:16.868 --> 00:10:18.467
-Okay. And are you just champing

00:10:18.467 --> 00:10:19.734
at the bit to get back at it?

00:10:19.734 --> 00:10:20.934
-I am. -Yeah.

00:10:20.934 --> 00:10:22.501
-When the show would be over off-Broadway --

00:10:22.501 --> 00:10:24.400
Daniel Radcliffe is in the show, as well.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.033
He's incredible. And Lindsay Mendez.

00:10:26.033 --> 00:10:29.033
[ Audience member whistles ] Yeah. Yeah, Dan. Alright.

00:10:29.033 --> 00:10:31.767
And Dan and I had shared a dressing room

00:10:31.767 --> 00:10:33.234
off-Broadway with Reg Rogers,

00:10:33.234 --> 00:10:36.400
and I would take off my pants, the costume pants.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.734
And I would be like, "I just want to put these back on

00:10:38.734 --> 00:10:40.734
and get back out there."

00:10:40.734 --> 00:10:43.801
And it was genuine. I'm dying to get back to it.

00:10:43.801 --> 00:10:45.200
-I am dying to see it.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.133
It's so exciting, and it's such a great cast.

00:10:47.133 --> 00:10:48.767
And, again, it's, you know, a musical.

